  "title": "Round spheres need one hidden bit per incidence",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Knot spaces of the two-kind law (every co-line has two or three points). (1) Every closed sphere of the law has exactly six digons, and with the local Kasteleyn sign rule (the signs at a co-line of d points have parity d + 1) its knot contradicts itself. (2) Tube lemma: for r >= 1, m >= 2r + 1 and L >= 2r + 1, every radius-r ball of the capped tube T(m, L, tau) is a radius-r ball of the octahedral sphere K_m, so a law of radius r without hidden states that allows K_m allows every such tube; the tube's knot still contradicts itself but is refuted by resolution in width O(m) at every length L. (3) Seam law: with one hidden bit per incidence and two rules at co-lines (S1: all four incidences of a digon are seams; S2: a co-line of three points has no seam or exactly one opposite pair), a closed two-kind sphere has a marking if and only if it is K_m for some m, and K_m has exactly one marking (its octahedron's edges). So the law's spheres are exactly the octahedral knot spheres, at every size, and each one's knot needs resolution width Theta(sqrt n) and size 2^Theta(sqrt n). (4) A growth law without branching grows at most one closed surface from a seed.",
